#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rjob — run heavy jobs on a remote host from the agent VM.

    rjob [--host H] [--project P] COMMAND ...
    rjob submit JOB.toml [--dry-run]               place, ship code + inputs, queue the job; prints job id
    rjob status ID | logs ID [N] | cancel ID | provenance ID
    rjob wait ID [--interval S]                    block until the job is finished; exit 0 iff done
    rjob fetch ID [--dest DIR] [--include GLOB]... resumable download of the job's outputs
    rjob hosts                                     capacity, load, cost and hour limit of every project host
    rjob cost [--since YYYY-MM-DD] [--host H]      hours and cost of recorded jobs, per job and per host
    rjob power status [ALIAS]                      cloud state, autostop decision, hold of power-managed hosts
    rjob power start ALIAS [--no-wait]             start a stopped cloud host, wait until its runner answers
    rjob power stop ALIAS [--cancel]               stop it once idle (the host decides; never kills jobs)
    rjob power hold ALIAS HOURS                    keep it up for up to 12 h (0 clears)
    rjob list [--all] | info                       jobs / info of one host (--all: every project host)
    rjob push REPO_PATH [REV]                      ship a commit (normally done by submit)
    rjob put-data FILE                             upload a large input once; prints sha256:...
    rjob cleanup job ID | cleanup builds DAYS

Hosts are ~/.ssh/config aliases whose key is locked to run-job.
Project: --project, else $RJOB_PROJECT, else `project = ...` in job.toml, else the
nearest rjob.toml above job.toml (or above the current directory). It lists the
project's hosts in order of preference and its default runs_dir. A host entry may
set cost_per_hour (default 0 = free) and max_hours (longest timeout_hours it should
take; a longer job goes there only when no other capable host is reachable).
Host of a new job: --host, else $RJOB_HOST, else `host = ...` in job.toml, else
placed on the first project host that can run its device and has room now, else on
the capable host with the shortest queue, passing over hosts whose max_hours is below
the job's timeout_hours while another capable host exists; without a project: "mac".
Commands on a job id use the host recorded at submit; --host overrides. A job
missing from the record: $RJOB_HOST, else the project host that knows the id,
else "mac".
Power: ~/.config/rjob/power.toml ($RJOB_POWER_CONFIG, mode 600) lists the cloud hosts
rjob may start, with their provider and instance (docs/power.md). The client only reads
state and starts; stops are decided by the host's autostop (request-stop), so a busy
host is never stopped through rjob.
$RJOB_SSH overrides the ssh command (tests).
Local record of submitted jobs: ~/.local/state/rjob/jobs.jsonl.
Python 3.11+ standard library only.
"""
